{'query': 'reputable peer-reviewed scientific source on causes and effects of climate change', 'results': [{'title': 'Causes and Effects of Climate Change | United Nations', 'url': 'https://www.un.org/en/climatechange/science/causes-effects-climate-change', 'snippet': 'This leads to global warming and climate change. The world is now warming faster than at any point in recorded history. Warmer temperatures over time are changing weather patterns and disrupting the usual balance of nature.'}, {'title': 'Causes of Climate Change | US EPA', 'url': 'https://www.epa.gov/climatechange-science/causes-climate-change', 'snippet': 'Causes of Climate Change. Natural processes are always influencing the earth’s climate and can explain climate changes prior to the Industrial Revolution in the 1700s.'}, {'title': "Isn't there a lot of disagreement among climate... | NOAA Climate.gov", 'url': 'https://www.climate.gov/news-features/climate-qa/isnt-there-lot-disagreement-among-climate-scientists-about-global-warming', 'snippet': "This finding hasn't changed with time. In 2016, a review paper summarized the results of several independent studies on peer-reviewed research related to climate. The authors found results consistent with a 97-percent consensus that human activity is causing climate change."}, {'title': 'What are the effects of global warming? | National Geographic', 'url': 'https://www.nationalgeographic.com/environment/article/global-warming-effects', 'snippet': 'Global warming and climate change are often used interchangeably as synonyms, but scientists prefer to use “climate change” when describing the complex shifts now affecting our planet’s weather and climate systems.'}, {'title': 'Climate Change - NASA Science', 'url': 'https://science.nasa.gov/climate-change/', 'snippet': 'What are the effects of climate change? The effects of human-caused global warming are happening now, are irreversible for people alive today, and will worsen as long as humans add greenhouse gases to the atmosphere.'}], 'result_count': 5, 'duration_seconds': 7.477384699974209}
{'query': 'government or international body report on causes and effects of climate change', 'results': [{'title': 'Environmental issues - Wikipedia', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Environmental_issues', 'snippet': 'Water pollution is an environmental issue that affects many water bodies. This photograph shows foam on the New River as it enters the United States from Mexico.The 2023 IPCC report highlighted the disproportionate effects of climate change on vulnerable populations.'}, {'title': 'Climate Change | United Nations', 'url': 'https://www.un.org/en/global-issues/climate-change', 'snippet': 'Climate change refers to long-term shifts in temperatures and weather patterns. Although some climate variations occur naturally, human activities have been the main driver of climate change since the 1800s, mainly through the burning of fossil fuels such as coal, oil, and gas.'}, {'title': 'What are the effects of global warming? | National Geographic', 'url': 'https://www.nationalgeographic.com/environment/article/global-warming-effects', 'snippet': 'Global warming and climate change are often used interchangeably as synonyms, but scientists prefer to use “climate change” when describing the complex shifts now affecting our planet’s weather and climate systems.'}, {'title': 'Causes of Climate Change | US EPA', 'url': 'https://www.epa.gov/climatechange-science/causes-climate-change', 'snippet': 'Burning fossil fuels changes the climate more than any other human activity.Causes of Climate Change. Natural processes are always influencing the earth’s climate and can explain climate changes prior to the Industrial Revolution in the 1700s.'}, {'title': 'NASA is a global leader in studying Earth’s changing climate.', 'url': 'https://science.nasa.gov/climate-change/', 'snippet': 'What are the effects of climate change? The effects of human-caused global warming are happening now, are irreversible for people alive today, and will worsen as long as humans add greenhouse gases to the atmosphere.'}], 'result_count': 5, 'duration_seconds': 4.867946400016081}
{'query': 'global average temperatures trend 2023 2024 latest data', 'results': [{'title': 'NASA Releases Global Temperature Data', 'url': 'https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasa-releases-global-temperature-data/', 'snippet': 'Jan 14, 2026 · Global temperatures in 2025 were cooler than 2024, with average temperatures of 2.14 degrees Fahrenheit (1.19 degrees Celsius) above the 1951 to 1980 average.'}, {'title': 'Global Temperature - Earth Indicator - NASA Science', 'url': 'https://science.nasa.gov/earth/explore/earth-indicators/global-temperature/', 'snippet': 'Jan 21, 2026 · Earth’s global surface temperature in 2025 was slightly warmer than 2023 – but within the margin of error: the two years are effectively tied according to an analysis by NASA scientists. Since record-keeping began in 1880, the hottest year on record remains 2024 (source: NASA/GISS).'}, {'title': 'NASA SVS | Record Temperature Years: 2025, 2024, and 2023', 'url': 'https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/5607', 'snippet': 'Feb 4, 2026 · A visualization of global temperature anomalies highlighting the record years of 2025, 2024, and 2023. The visualizations morphs between a data grid showing monthly temperatures and a bar chart of annual temperatures.'}, {'title': 'Global Climate Highlights 2024 | Copernicus', 'url': 'https://climate.copernicus.eu/global-climate-highlights-2024', 'snippet': 'Jan 10, 2025 · 2024 was the warmest year in a multi-dataset record of global temperature going back to 1850. 2024 had a global average temperature of 15.10°C; 0.12°C higher than the previous highest annual value in 2023.'}, {'title': 'Climate change: global temperature - NOAA Climate.gov', 'url': 'https://www.climate.gov/news-features/understanding-climate/climate-change-global-temperature', 'snippet': 'May 29, 2025 · Earth’s temperature has risen by an average of 0.11° Fahrenheit (0.06° Celsius) per decade since 1850, or about 2° F in total. The rate of warming since 1982 is more than three times as fast: 0.36° F (0.20° C) per decade. 2024 was the warmest year since global records began in 1850 by a wide margin.'}], 'result_count': 5, 'duration_seconds': 5.740263000014238}
